question:
Suppose that, for some angles $x$ and $y$,

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \sin ^{2} x+\cos ^{2} y=\frac{3}{2} a \\
& \cos ^{2} x+\sin ^{2} y=\frac{1}{2} a^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Determine the possible value(s) of $a$.

response:
We have \begin{align*}
f(x)&=\sin ^{6} x+\cos ^{6} x+k\left(\sin ^{4} x+\cos ^{4} x\right)\\
&=\left(\sin ^{2} x+\cos ^{2} x\right)\left(\sin ^{4} x-\sin ^{2} x \cos ^{2} x+\cos ^{4} x\right)\\
&\qquad+k\left(\left(\sin ^{2} x+\cos ^{2} x\right)^{2}-2 \sin ^{2} x \cos ^{2} x\right)\\
&=\sin ^{4} x-\sin ^{2} x \cos ^{2} x+\cos ^{4} x+k-2 k \sin ^{2} x \cos ^{2} x\\
&=\left(\sin ^{2} x+\cos ^{2} x\right)^{2}-3 \sin ^{2} x \cos ^{2} x+k-2 k \sin ^{2} x \cos ^{2} x\\
&=1+(k-3) \sin ^{2} x \cos ^{2} x\\
&=1+\frac{k-3}{4} \sin ^{2} 2 x.
\end{align*}We can rewrite the equation $f(x)=0$ as \[1+\frac{k-3}{4} \sin ^{2} 2 x=0.\]This equation can be written as \[\sin ^{2} 2 x=\frac{-4}{k-3}.\]Since $0 \leq \sin ^{2} 2 x \leq 1$, we must have $0 \leq \frac{-4}{k-3} \leq 1$. We have two cases to consider.

Case 1: $k-3<0$. Then we have $0 \leq \frac{-4}{k-3} \leq 1 \iff 0 \leq \frac{-4}{k-3} \leq \frac{-4}{k-3} \iff 0 \leq 1$, which is true for all $k<3$.

Case 2: $k-3>0$. Then we have $0 \leq \frac{-4}{k-3} \leq 1 \iff 0 \leq \frac{4}{3-k} \leq \frac{4}{3-k} \iff 0 \leq 1$, which is true for all $k>3$.

Case 3: $k-3=0$. Then the equation $f(x)=0$ becomes $1=0$, which has no solutions.

Combining Case 1 and Case 2, we have that the values of $k$ for which there exists a real number $c$ such that $f(c)=0$ are all real numbers $k \neq 3$.

