The hexagon has 6 vertices, so the number of triangles is $\binom{6}{3} = 20$.

The vertices of the hexagon can be represented as the complex numbers $1, \omega, \omega^2, \omega^3, \omega^4, \omega^5$ where $\omega = e^{i\pi/3}$.

Let $a, b, c$ be three of these vertices, and let $\Delta$ be the area of the triangle $\triangle abc$. Then

\begin{align*}
\Delta &= \frac{1}{2} \left| \text{Im} \left( (b-a)\overline{(c-a)} \right) \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left| \text{Im} \left( (b-a)(\overline{c}-\overline{a}) \right) \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left| \text{Im} \left( b\overline{c} - b\overline{a} - a\overline{c} + a\overline{a} \right) \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left| \text{Im} \left( b\overline{c} - b\overline{a} - a\overline{c} \right) \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left| \text{Im} \left( b\overline{c} \right) + \text{Im} \left( a\overline{b} \right) + \text{Im} \left( c\overline{a} \right) \right|
\end{align*}

The average area is

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{20} \sum_{a,b,c} \Delta &= \frac{1}{20} \sum_{a,b,c} \frac{1}{2} \left| \text{Im} \left( b\overline{c} \right) + \text{Im} \left( a\overline{b} \right) + \text{Im} \left( c\overline{a} \right) \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{40} \sum_{a,b,c} \left| \text{Im} \left( b\overline{c} \right) + \text{Im} \left( a\overline{b} \right) + \text{Im} \left( c\overline{a} \right) \right|
\end{align*}

Let's consider the expression $b\overline{c}$. Since $b$ and $c$ are vertices of the hexagon, they are of the form $\omega^k$ for some integer $k$. Thus,

\begin{align*}
b\overline{c} &= \omega^k \overline{\omega^j} \\
&= \omega^k \omega^{-j} \\
&= \omega^{k-j}
\end{align*}

The imaginary part of this expression is $\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3}(k-j)\right)$. Since $k$ and $j$ are integers, the imaginary part can only be $0, \pm\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$.

Furthermore, the expression $b\overline{c}$ is symmetric with respect to $b$ and $c$. This means that if $b\overline{c}$ is $\omega^{k-j}$, then $c\overline{b}$ is $\omega^{-(k-j)}$. Therefore,

\begin{align*}
\text{Im} \left( b\overline{c} \right) + \text{Im} \left( c\overline{b} \right) &= 0
\end{align*}

This means that when we sum over all $a,b,c$, the terms $\text{Im} \left( b\overline{c} \right) + \text{Im} \left( c\overline{b} \right)$ cancel out. Therefore,

\begin{align*}
\sum_{a,b,c} \left| \text{Im} \left( b\overline{c} \right) + \text{Im} \left( a\overline{b} \right) + \text{Im} \left( c\overline{a} \right) \right| &= \sum_{a,b,c} \left| \text{Im} \left( a\overline{b} \right) \right|
\end{align*}

Since $a$ and $b$ are vertices of the hexagon, they are of the form $\omega^k$ for some integer $k$. Thus,

\begin{align*}
\left| \text{Im} \left( a\overline{b} \right) \right| &= \left| \text{Im} \left( \omega^k \overline{\omega^j} \right) \right| \\
&= \left| \text{Im} \left( \omega^{k-j} \right) \right| \\
&= \left| \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3}(k-j)\right) \right|
\end{align*}

The term $\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3}(k-j)\right)$ can be $\pm\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$ when $k-j$ is not divisible by 3, and 0 otherwise. There are $\binom{6}{2} = 15$ pairs of vertices, and for each pair, there are 4 choices for $c$. Therefore,

\begin{align*}
\sum_{a,b,c} \left| \text{Im} \left( a\overline{b} \right) \right| &= 4 \sum_{a,b} \left| \text{Im} \left( a\overline{b} \right) \right| \\
&= 4 \cdot 15 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \\
&= 30\sqrt{3}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the average area is

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{40} \sum_{a,b,c} \left| \text{Im} \left( b\overline{c} \right) + \text{Im} \left( a\overline{b} \right) + \text{Im} \left( c\overline{a} \right) \right| &= \frac{1}{40} \sum_{a,b,c} \left| \text{Im} \left( a\overline{b} \right) \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{40} \cdot 30\sqrt{3} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{4}}
\end{align*}
