By the change-of-base formula,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\log _{a} c&=\frac{\log c}{\log a}=\frac{1}{\log _{c} a}=T \\
\log _{b} d&=\frac{\log d}{\log b}=\frac{1}{\log _{d} b}=T
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, $\log _{c} a=\frac{1}{T}$ and $\log _{d} b=\frac{1}{T}$.  We can use these to compute

$$
\begin{aligned}
\log _{\sqrt{a b}}(c d)^{3}&=\frac{\log (c d)^{3}}{\log \sqrt{a b}}=\frac{3(\log c+\log d)}{\frac{1}{2}(\log a+\log b)} \\
&=\frac{6(\log c+\log d)}{\log a+\log b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Using the change-of-base formula again, we get

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\log _{\sqrt{a b}}(c d)^{3}}{\log _{a} c+\log _{b} d}&=\frac{\frac{6(\log c+\log d)}{\log a+\log b}}{\frac{\log c}{\log a}+\frac{\log d}{\log b}} \\
&=\frac{\frac{6(\log c+\log d)}{\log a+\log b}}{\frac{\log c\log b+\log d\log a}{\log a\log b}} \\
&=\frac{6(\log c+\log d)(\log a\log b)}{(\log a+\log b)(\log c\log b+\log d\log a)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiplying both the numerator and denominator by $\log c\log d$ gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\log _{\sqrt{a b}}(c d)^{3}}{\log _{a} c+\log _{b} d}&=\frac{6(\log c+\log d)(\log a\log b)(\log c\log d)}{(\log a+\log b)(\log c\log b+\log d\log a)(\log c\log d)} \\
&=\frac{6(\log c+\log d)(\log a\log b)(\log c\log d)}{(\log a+\log b)(\log c\log d\log b+\log c\log d\log a)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Factoring out $\log c\log d$ from the denominator gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\log _{\sqrt{a b}}(c d)^{3}}{\log _{a} c+\log _{b} d}&=\frac{6(\log c+\log d)(\log a\log b)(\log c\log d)}{(\log a+\log b)\log c\log d(\log b+\log a)} \\
&=\frac{6(\log c+\log d)(\log a\log b)}{(\log a+\log b)^{2}} \\
&=\frac{6(\log c+\log d)}{\log a+\log b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Finally, we substitute $\log c=\frac{1}{T}\log a$ and $\log d=\frac{1}{T}\log b$ to obtain

$$
\frac{6(\log c+\log d)}{\log a+\log b}=\frac{6(\frac{1}{T}\log a+\frac{1}{T}\log b)}{\log a+\log b}=\frac{6}{T}
$$

Therefore, the answer is $\boxed{\frac{6}{T}}$.
